# ---------------- 1 Overview (slides 1-3)
add("1","reuther2022survey","Reuther, Michaleas, Jones, Gadepally, Samsi and Kepner (MIT Lincoln Laboratory)",2022,"AI and ML accelerator survey and trends (IEEE HPEC 2022)","2210.04055",None,"Survey paper. The fourth annual scatter of peak operations per second against power for published accelerators, with release dates and the neuromorphic, photonic and memristive entries; the model for the landscape chart. Slides 2 and 3.")
add("1","reuther2021survey","Reuther, Michaleas, Jones, Gadepally, Samsi and Kepner (MIT Lincoln Laboratory)",2021,"AI accelerator survey and trends (IEEE HPEC 2021)","2109.08957",None,"Survey paper. The previous year's edition, with the 2016 to 2021 wave of startups (Graphcore, Groq, SambaNova, Cerebras) on one chart; read it to see which entries disappeared by 2026. Slides 2 and 3.")
add("1","hennessy2019golden","Hennessy and Patterson",2019,"A new golden age for computer architecture (Communications of the ACM 62(2), Turing lecture)",None,"https://cacm.acm.org/?p=7368","Primary paper (lecture). The end of Dennard scaling and Moore's law as the case for domain-specific architectures, with the TPU as the worked example; the thesis behind every program on the who's-who table. Slides 1 and 3.")
add("1","sastry2026atpesc","Sastry and Emani (Argonne)",2026,"ALCF AI Testbed (ATPESC 2026 slides, August 4, 2026)",None,"https://extremecomputingtraining.anl.gov/sites/atpesc/files/2026-08/AI%20Testbeds.pdf","Laboratory slides. The non-GPU machines one DOE facility still runs in 2026: 4 CS-3, SN40L nodes with 16 RDUs each, 2 Tenstorrent Galaxy nodes, a 72-chip GroqRack and a Bow Pod64. Slides 3 and 35.")
add("1","alcf2026testbed","Argonne Leadership Computing Facility",2026,"ALCF AI Testbed (system page)",None,"https://ai.alcf.anl.gov","Facility page. The testbed inventory: GroqRack (72 chips), Cerebras CS-2, SambaNova DataScale (64 RDUs), Graphcore Bow Pod64, Habana Gaudi; the systems behind the ALCF evaluations cited later. Slides 3 and 32.")
add("1","hotchips2026program","Hot Chips",2026,"Hot Chips 2026 conference program (August 23-25, 2026)",None,"https://hc2026.hotchips.org/program/conference/","Conference program. Where the 2026 accelerators were presented, including the Samsung LPDDR5X-PIM talk; the dates behind the timeline. Slides 3 and 28.")
add("1","sth2025hotchips","ServeTheHome",2025,"Hot Chips 2025 preliminary schedule released",None,"https://www.servethehome.com/hot-chips-2025-preliminary-schedule-released/","Press, secondary source. The 2025 machine-learning sessions: Google Ironwood, d-Matrix Corsair, AMD MI350 and NVIDIA GB10 on one day; who was presenting non-GPU silicon that year. Slides 3, 11 and 28.")
add("1","register2026vc","The Register",2026,"AI chip startups soak up $1.1B in VC funding this week (February 25, 2026)",None,"https://www.theregister.com/2026/02/25/ai_chips_vc_funding_1point1billion/","Press, secondary source. MatX ($500M Series B), Axelera (over $250M) and SambaNova ($350M with an Intel collaboration) in one week; the 2026 funding status on the who's-who table. Slides 3 and 34.")

# ---------------- 2 Principles (slides 4-8)
add("2","kung1982systolic","Kung",1982,"Why systolic architectures? (IEEE Computer 15(1))",None,"https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~smk9u/CS6501F19/kung_-_1982_-_why_systolic_architectures.pdf","Primary paper (course mirror). The origin of the systolic-array vocabulary: data pulsing through a grid of cells so each operand fetched is used many times; the idea behind the MXU. Slides 5 and 7.")
add("2","horowitz2014energy","Horowitz",2014,"Computing's energy problem (and what we can do about it) (ISSCC 2014, paper 1.1)",None,"https://www.Gwern.net/doc/cs/hardware/2014-horowitz-2.pdf","Primary paper (mirror). The 45 nm energy table: adds and multiplies in fractions of a pJ to a few pJ, cache reads of 10 to 100 pJ, a DRAM access of 1 to 2 nJ; the bar chart on the SRAM slide. Slides 4 and 8.")
add("2","dally2016berkeley","Dally (NVIDIA and Stanford)",2016,"High-performance hardware for machine learning (UC Berkeley CS294 lecture, October 19, 2016)",None,"https://berkeley-deep-learning.github.io/cs294-dl-f16/slides/DL_HW_Berkeley_0916.pdf","Lecture slides. A legible reproduction of the Horowitz table (32-bit FP multiply 3.7 pJ, 8 KB SRAM read 5 pJ, 32-bit DRAM read 640 pJ) and the argument for keeping data on chip. Slide 8.")
add("2","dally2020dsa","Dally, Turakhia and Han",2020,"Domain-specific hardware accelerators (Communications of the ACM 63(7))",None,"https://www.doc.ic.ac.uk/~wl/teachlocal/arch/papers/cacm20dsa.pdf","Primary paper (course mirror). The four sources of accelerator gain and the updated energy costs: an add instruction about 4,000 times the add, 8 KB SRAM 50 fJ/bit, 100 MB on chip 0.7 pJ/bit, DDR4 about 20 pJ/bit, SerDes about 10 pJ/bit. Slides 6, 8 and 30.")
add("2","gholami2024memwall","Gholami, Yao, Kim, Hooper, Mahoney and Keutzer",2024,"AI and memory wall (IEEE Micro 2024)","2403.14123",None,"Primary paper. Peak server FLOP/s grew 3.0 times every two years, DRAM bandwidth 1.6 and interconnect 1.4; the three exponentials on the memory-wall slide. Slide 4.")
add("2","valiant1990bsp","Valiant",1990,"A bridging model for parallel computation (Communications of the ACM 33(8))",None,"https://www.osti.gov/biblio/6502724","Bibliographic record (paper paywalled). The bulk-synchronous parallel model of compute, communicate and synchronize supersteps, which Graphcore's IPU implements literally. Slides 5 and 18.")
add("2","chen2016eyeriss","Chen, Emer and Sze",2016,"Eyeriss: a spatial architecture for energy-efficient dataflow for convolutional neural networks (ISCA 2016)",None,"https://sands.kaust.edu.sa/classes/CS290E/F19/papers/eyeriss.pdf","Primary paper (course mirror). Defines the weight-stationary, output-stationary, no-local-reuse and row-stationary dataflows, the vocabulary for where operands wait in each machine. Slides 5 and 7.")
add("2","sze2017survey","Sze, Chen, Yang and Emer",2017,"Efficient processing of deep neural networks: a tutorial and survey (Proceedings of the IEEE)","1703.09039",None,"Survey paper. Dataflows, memory hierarchy and reduced precision organized in one place, with the energy-per-access hierarchy from registers to DRAM; the best single introduction to this section. Slides 4, 5 and 8.")
add("2","prabhakar2017plasticine","Prabhakar, Zhang, Koeplinger, Feldman, Zhao, Hadjis, Pedram, Kozyrakis and Olukotun",2017,"Plasticine: a reconfigurable architecture for parallel patterns (ISCA 2017)",None,"https://sands.kaust.edu.sa/classes/CS290E/F19/papers/plasticine.pdf","Primary paper (course mirror). The coarse-grained reconfigurable array of pattern compute and memory units, 113 mm2 at 28 nm, 49 W, up to 76.9 times an FPGA's perf/W; the academic ancestor of SambaNova's RDU. Slides 5 and 17.")
add("2","fowers2018brainwave","Fowers, Ovtcharov, Papamichael, ... Chung and Burger (Microsoft)",2018,"A configurable cloud-scale DNN processor for real-time AI (ISCA 2018)",None,"https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/a-configurable-cloud-scale-dnn-processor-for-real-time-ai/","Primary paper (publication page). Project Brainwave: an FPGA soft NPU with weights pinned in on-chip memory, 10 to over 35 TFLOP/s at batch 1 on Stratix 10; the first data-center SRAM-resident serving design and Microsoft's step before Maia. Slides 8 and 22.")
add("2","shilov2022sram","Shilov (Tom's Hardware)",2022,"TSMC's 3nm node: no SRAM scaling implies more expensive CPUs and GPUs (December 16, 2022)",None,"https://tomshardware.com/news/no-sram-scaling-implies-on-more-expensive-cpus-and-gpus","Press, secondary source. The SRAM bitcell stays at about 0.021 um2 from N5 to N3E, so on-chip capacity no longer gets cheaper with each node; why SRAM-only designs hit a capacity wall. Slide 8.")
add("2","epoch2026hbmcost","Somala (Epoch AI)",2026,"Memory has grown to nearly two-thirds of AI chip component costs (May 21, 2026)",None,"https://epoch.ai/data-insights/ai-chip-component-cost-shares","Analyst estimate, secondary source. HBM rose from 52 to 63 percent of accelerator component spending between Q1 2024 and Q4 2025, logic dies about 13 percent; the cost side of the SRAM-versus-HBM trade. Slides 4 and 8.")

# ---------------- 3 The Google TPU (slides 9-12)
add("3","jouppi2017tpu","Jouppi, Young, Patil, Patterson et al. (Google)",2017,"In-datacenter performance analysis of a tensor processing unit (ISCA 2017)","1704.04760",None,"Primary paper. TPU v1: a 256x256 8-bit MXU (65,536 MACs), 92 TOPS at 700 MHz, 24 MiB unified buffer, 8 GiB DDR3 at 34 GB/s, 75 W, 28 nm; 15 to 30 times a K80 and 30 to 80 times its perf/W, with the roofline showing DDR3 as the limit. Slides 6, 7 and 9.")
add("3","norrie2020hc32","Norrie, Patil, Yoon, Kurian, Li, Laudon, Young, Jouppi and Patterson (Google)",2020,"Google's training chips revealed: TPUv2 and TPUv3 (Hot Chips 32 slides)",None,"https://www.hc32.hotchips.org/assets/program/conference/day2/HotChips2020_ML_Training_Google_Norrie_Patil.v01.pdf","Primary conference slides (the talk behind Norrie et al., IEEE Micro 41(2), 2021). Why 128x128: about 1.6 times the utilization of one 256x256 array at constant FLOP/s; 256-chip 11.5 PF and 1,024-chip 100+ PF pods, 4 ICI links of 500 Gb/s in a 2D torus. Slides 7 and 9.")
add("3","norrie2021micro","Norrie, Patil, Yoon, Kurian, Li, Laudon, Young, Jouppi and Patterson (Google)",2021,"The design process for Google's training chips: TPUv2 and TPUv3 (IEEE Micro 41(2))",None,"https://www.Gwern.net/doc/ai/scaling/hardware/2021-norrie.pdf","Primary paper (mirror). TPU v2 as a different machine from v1: two TensorCores with VLIW scalar units driving vector units and 128 x 128 MXUs (32,768 operations per cycle at 700 MHz), BF16, HBM at 700 GB/s (20 times v1's DDR3), and the ICI torus. Slides 7 and 9.")
add("3","jouppi2021tpuv4i","Jouppi, Yoon, Ashcraft, Gottscho et al. (Google)",2021,"Ten lessons from three generations shaped Google's TPUv4i (ISCA 2021, industry track)",None,"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~18742/papers/Jouppi2021.pdf","Primary paper (course mirror). TPUv4i: 7 nm, 138 TFLOP/s BF16, 128 MB of CMEM chosen as the knee of the curve, 175 W chip TDP; and the ten design lessons, including why TPU v2 went to two cores. Slides 7 and 10.")
add("3","jouppi2023tpuv4","Jouppi, Kurian, Li, Ma, Nagarajan, Nai, Patil, Subramanian, Swing, Towles, Young, Zhou, Zhou and Patterson (Google)",2023,"TPU v4: an optically reconfigurable supercomputer for machine learning with hardware support for embeddings (ISCA 2023)","2304.01433",None,"Primary paper. 4,096 chips in 64 cubes joined by optical circuit switches (under 5 percent of cost and under 3 percent of power), SparseCores at 5 to 7 times on embeddings, 1.2 to 1.7 times an A100. Slide 10.")
add("3","jouppi2026fivegen","Jouppi, Lakshmanamurthy, Young and Patterson (Google)",2026,"Google's training supercomputers from TPU v2 to Ironwood: architectural stability, scale, resilience, power efficiency, and sustainability across five generations (IEEE Micro)","2606.15870",None,"Primary paper. Peak per chip up about 100 times (46 TFLOP/s BF16 to 4,614 FP8), HBM about 10 times, pods from 256 to 9,216 chips, peak perf per TDP watt about 30 times (Ironwood 6 times v5p), 93 percent goodput training Gemini 2.5. Slides 9 to 11.")
add("3","jouppi2025ironwoodhc","Jouppi and Lakshmanamurthy (Google)",2025,"Ironwood: delivering best in class perf, perf/TCO and perf/Watt for reasoning model training and serving (Hot Chips 2025 slides)",None,"https://hc2025.hotchips.org/assets/program/conference/day2/61_Google_Ironwood-Final.pdf","Primary conference slides. The two-die TPU: 4,614 TFLOP/s FP8, 192 GiB HBM3E in 8 stacks at 7.3 TB/s, 1.2 TB/s ICI, 9,216 chips through OCS at 42.5 EFLOP/s FP8 with 1.77 PB of shared HBM. Slide 11.")
add("3","googletpu7x","Google Cloud",2026,"TPU7x (Ironwood) system architecture (documentation)",None,"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/tpu7x","Vendor documentation. The official per-chip row: 2,307 TFLOP/s BF16, 4,614 FP8, 192 GiB at 7,380 GB/s, 1,200 GB/s bidirectional ICI, 4x4x4 cubes up to 9,216 chips. Slides 11 and 31.")
add("3","googletpuv6e","Google Cloud",2025,"TPU v6e (Trillium) documentation",None,"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/v6e","Vendor documentation. 918 TFLOP/s BF16, 1,836 TOPS INT8, 32 GB at 1,638 GB/s, 800 GB/s ICI, 256-chip 2D torus. Slide 11.")
add("3","googletpuv5p","Google Cloud",2025,"TPU v5p documentation",None,"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/v5p","Vendor documentation. 459 TFLOP/s BF16, 95 GiB usable of 96 GiB HBM at 2,765 GB/s, 1,200 GB/s ICI, 8,960-chip 3D torus. Slide 11.")
add("3","googletpuv5e","Google Cloud",2025,"TPU v5e documentation",None,"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/v5e","Vendor documentation. 197 TFLOP/s BF16, 393 TOPS INT8, 16 GB at 800 GiB/s, 400 GB/s ICI, 256-chip 2D torus; the first inference-line TPU. Slide 11.")
add("3","google2024trilliumga","Lohmeyer (Google Cloud)",2024,"Announcing the general availability of Trillium, our sixth-generation TPU (December 11, 2024)",None,"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/trillium-tpu-is-ga","Vendor blog. 4.7 times peak per chip, double the HBM and ICI, 67 percent better energy efficiency; Gemini 2.0 trained on it; GA date on the business timeline. Slides 11 and 12.")
add("3","google2024trilliummlperf","Pichika and Singh (Google Cloud)",2024,"Trillium MLPerf 4.1 training benchmarks (November 14, 2024)",None,"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/trillium-mlperf-41-training-benchmarks","Vendor blog. GPT-3 175B in MLPerf Training 4.1 (preview): 99 percent scaling efficiency across Trillium pods with Multislice and up to 1.8 times the perf per dollar of v5p. Slides 11, 12 and 32.")
add("3","google2025ironwoodga","Google Cloud",2025,"Announcing Ironwood TPUs general availability and new Axion VMs (November 6, 2025)",None,"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/ironwood-tpus-and-new-axion-based-vms-for-your-ai-workloads/","Vendor blog. Ironwood GA in the coming weeks, 10 times v5p peak, 9,216-chip superpods, and Anthropic's plan to access up to one million TPUs. Slides 11 and 12.")
add("3","storagereview2026tpu8","StorageReview",2026,"Google announces TPU 8t Sunfish and TPU 8i Zebrafish (May 1, 2026)",None,"https://www.storagereview.com/news/google-announces-tpu-8t-sunfish-and-tpu-8i-zebrafish","Press, secondary source. The TPU 8 columns: 8t at 12.6 PF FP4, 216 GB at 6.5 TB/s, 9,600-chip superpods; 8i at 10.1 PF FP4, 288 GB at 8.6 TB/s, 384 MB SRAM, the Boardfly network. Slides 11 and 31.")

# ---------------- 4 Cerebras and the wafer (slides 13-15)
add("4","lie2023micro","Lie (Cerebras)",2023,"Cerebras architecture deep dive: first look inside the hardware/software co-design for deep learning (IEEE Micro 43(3); author's blog version)",None,"https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/cerebras-architecture-deep-dive-first-look-inside-the-hw-sw-co-design-for-deep-learning","Primary paper (author's web version, with a link to the IEEE Micro issue). The core: 48 KB of SRAM in eight banks, a four-FMAC FP16 datapath, dataflow triggering; weight streaming. The WSE-2's 7.5 PF FP16 used on slides 2, 13 and 31 is the figure analysts quote; the fact-check found no dense/sparse qualifier at its source. Slides 13 and 14.")
add("4","rocki2020stencil","Rocki, Van Essendelft, Sharapov, Schreiber, Morrison, Kibardin, Portnoy, Dietiker, Syamlal and James",2020,"Fast stencil-code computation on a wafer-scale processor (SC20)","2010.03660",None,"Primary paper. A bandwidth-bound 3D stencil from NETL runs near peak on a CS-1; the evidence that the wafer behaves as one device with PB/s of local bandwidth, and the reason labs bought the first units. Slides 13 and 14.")
